  {
    "id": "rule-strong-verb-ablaut",
    "title": "Strong verbs: shared vowel-change pattern (ablaut)",
    "description": "Many of the most common German and English verbs are 'strong verbs' — instead of adding '-ed'/'-te', they change their core vowel to form past tenses, and German and English often use the same vowel pattern because both descend from the same Proto-Germanic strong verb classes.",
    "type": "cognate-pair",
    "examples": [
